Usage Instructions for the Xerox 820 AX.25 TNC Code by KA9Q

The commands are as follows:
1. reset
Do a soft reset. Configuration parameters are unaffected, but any
established links are dropped.

2. load [file]
Load configuration data from disk. Default is "PARAM.PAD" on the currently
logged-in disk, but an optional file name may be specified. This command
involves a reset, so it drops any active links. Warning: loading a "garbage"
file will cause strange things to happen!

3. save [file]
Save configuration information on disk. Default is "PARAM.PAD" on the
currently logged-in disk, but an alternate file may be specified.  The
information that is saved is as follows:

Terminal parameters
Default link parameters
Number of HDLC lines
HDLC line parameters

4. connect <lineno> <call> [<digipeater>]
Establish an AX.25 link with <call> via HDLC line number <lineno>, using
optionally specified digipeaters. Note that the HDLC line number MUST be
specified. There is no "via" clause between the destination and the
digipeater string, if any.  When the connection is established, the terminal
does NOT automatically become connected to the station; the "trans" or
"convers" commands must then be used.

Here are some examples (with comments in [brackets]):
connect 0 wa2lqq	[initiate a direct connection to wa2lqq via line 0]
connect 1 w2fpy wa2lqq	[connect to w2fpy via wa2lqq on line 1]

5. disc wa2lqq
Initiate a link disconnect with wa2lqq.

6. purge
Clear the link table of all entries in the disconnected state. Entries
in any other state are unaffected. Link table entries remain indefinitely
until this command is given.

Parameter Display/Set Commands

Valid parameters for these commands are of several types:

"Characters" are the actual characters, typed in a single keystroke.
On output, they are displayed as multi-character sequences (e.g, ^C)
to make them readable.

"Integers" are numbers between 0 and 65535 inclusive.

"Time" units are integers representing units of 100 ms (0.1 sec).

"On/Off" are the actual characters "On" and "Off", although the integers
1 and 0 may also be used.

For trace control commands, there are three values: 0 - no trace output;
1 - short trace (decode frame header); 2 - long trace (dump entire frame
in hex and ASCII).

7. linkparam [call | default] param value
This command does different things depending on how many arguments are given.

a. linkparam
Status and statistics for each link in the link table is displayed.

b. linkparam wa2lqq
The list of configurable parameters for the connection to wa2lqq
is displayed. Valid parameters follow (these are distinct from
the information given in case 'a' above).

ackdel		(time) I frame acknowledgement delay (AX.25 timer T1)
frack		(time) Acknowledgement timer (AX.25 timer T2)
idlepoll	(time) Idle link keep-alive poll timer (AX.25 timer T3)


maxframe	(1-7) Maximum number of outstanding I-frames
newproto	(on/off) If set ON, use P/F bits in AX.25 level 2 during
		recovery; if set OFF, use I-frame retransmission recovery
retry		(integer) Retry count limit

tfrmr		(0/1/2) Trace FRMR frames
tstate		(0/1/2) Trace state transitions
ttimer		(0/1/2) Trace link timer events

c. linkparam wa2lqq retry
Only the value of the "retry" parameter for the connection to wa2lqq
is displayed (case 'b' displays all parameters.)

d. linkparam wa2lqq retry 5
Sets the value of the "retry" parameter for the connection to wa2lqq to 5.

In cases b, c and d, the reserved name "default" may be used as a call sign;
this allows display and modification of the default link parameters to be used
whenever a new link entry is created. Example:

linkparam default retry 5

causes all new links to be established with a retry parameter of 5.
Note that existing link table entries are unaffected, even those in
the disconnected state.

8. lineparam lineno param value
This command also takes between 0 and 3 arguments.

a. lineparam
Displays state and traffic statistics for each HDLC line.

b. lineparam 0
Displays the list of configurable parameters for line 0. This information
is distinct from that displayed in 'a' above:

digipeat	(on/off) Enable/disable digipeating.
mode		(0/1/2) 0: Carrier Sense Multiple Access (CSMA)
			Defer transmitting when receiving carrier
			1: Aloha - transmit at will, ignore receiver carrier
			2: Full duplex - transmit continuously
psize		(integer) Buffer allocation size for receive frames. Any
		incoming frames larger than psize will be treated as
		erroneous and ignored.
speed		(integer) Set line speed. The following speeds are
		theoretically supported by the 820: 800, 1200, 1760, 2144,
		2400, 4800, 9600, 19200, 28800, 32000, 38400, 57600. However,
		real-time constraints keep a 2.5 mhz 820 from operating
		faster than 2400 baud in a full-duplex loopback mode. I'm
		working	on this...
tin		(0/1/2) Control tracing of incoming frames
tout		(0/1/2) Control tracing of outgoing frames
tused		(0/1/2) Control tracing of frames addressed to this station
txdelay		(integer) Transmitter keyup delay (100 ms increments)

c. lineparam 0 speed
Displays only the line speed for HDLC line 0.

d. lineparam 0 speed 1200
Sets the speed of HDLC line 0 to 1200 bps. Also displays the previous
line speed.

Terminal session mode commands

9.conv wa2lqq
Enter conversational mode with station wa2lqq. The station must be
present in the link table. If the link state is connected, normal
I-frames will be sent; if the link state is disconnected, UI frames
are sent.

10. trans wa2lqq
Enter transparent mode with wa2lqq. Otherwise the same as the 'conv'
command.

11. memory
Display memory usage statistics.

12. mycall ka9q
Set the identity of this station to "ka9q". Without parameters, mycall
displays the current setting of the station address.

13. term 
Display and/or change the following terminal parameters. Note that many
of these are either no-ops on the 820, or are not yet really implemented.

blen	(integer)	Number of bits/character to terminal. Noop on 820.
bits	(integer)	Number of stop bits to terminal. Noop on 820.
canline	(character)	Line cancel character.
canpac	(character)	Packet cancel character. Not implemented. It was
			never really clear to me that this is useful.
command	(character)	Command recall character.
cr	(on/off)	Control adding cr to packets. Not implemented.
			This feature needs to be thought out more.
delete	(character)	Character delete character.
echo	(on/off)	Controls echoing of typed input.
flow	(on/off)	Inhibit output while typing. Not implemented.
hiwat	(integer)	Flow control point on keyboard buffer.
lowat	(integer)	Flow control restart point on keyboard buffer.
parity	(integer)	Parity control on terminal. Noop on 820.
pass	(character)	Pass-through ("quoting") character. Not implemented.
sendpac	(character)	Character which terminates commands and packets.
speed	(integer)	Serial line speed. Noop on 820.
start	(character)	Flow control restart character, user -> 820
stop	(character)	Flow control stop character, user -> 820
xoff	(character)	Flow control stop character, 820 -> user (not impl.)
xon	(character)	Flow control restart char, 820 -> user (not impl.)

a. term 
Display the complete list.

b. term speed
Display only the terminal speed.

c. term speed 1200
Sets the terminal speed to 1200 baud. (Note that this is actually a
no-op for the Xerox 820, as are several other terminal parameters,
since the parallel keyboard and memory mapped display are used.)

Summary

The amount of work which the command interpreter code required surprised me.
I am not completely satisfied with it, because it ought to be possible to
do more cleanly than this. I am particularly interested in user feedback
regarding the command set. Are there features missing that you really need?
Useless commands that should be deleted to save space? Is the parser
adequate? Are the commands reasonably named? etc.
